{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794314620853,"sessionID":"ses_e3a86b1d7ffefMSIuRfahpJxKE","part":{"id":"prt_1c57c4f9b001PfI6sKI46x5L1S","messageID":"msg_1c57c2242001isEmnzeBy9RXEz","sessionID":"ses_e3a86b1d7ffefMSIuRfahpJxKE","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794314620853,"sessionID":"ses_e3a86b1d7ffefMSIuRfahpJxKE","part":{"id":"prt_1c57c4f9f001rwJPtCiG0WMj6Y","messageID":"msg_1c57c2242001isEmnzeBy9RXEz","sessionID":"ses_e3a86b1d7ffefMSIuRfahpJxKE","type":"text","text":"<think>Now let me write the frontend. Files:\n\n- frontend/src/index.css — tailwind import + a couple of base styles (allowed: \"avoid hand-written CSS files except for Tailwind's own entry file\" — index.css is Tailwind's entry file, so fine).\n- frontend/src/main.tsx — BrowserRouter + App\n- frontend/src/api.ts — API client\n- frontend/src/auth.tsx — auth context\n- frontend/src/App.tsx — layout + routes\n- frontend/src/pages/*.tsx\n\nLet me design a nice-looking UI with Tailwind utility classes.\n\nDesign: light slate background, white cards, indigo accents. Header with app name, nav links (Home/Account/Mailbox), right side shows username + Sign out.\n\napi.ts:\n\n```ts\nexport type Account = { id: number; username: string; email: string | null };\nexport type OutboxMessage = { id: number; to: string; subject: string; body: string; sent_at: string };\n\nconst TOKEN_KEY = \"auth.token\";\n\nexport function getToken(): string | null { return localStorage.getItem(TOKEN_KEY); }\nexport function setToken(token: string | null) { ... }\n\nasync function request<T>(path: string, init: RequestInit = {}): Promise<T> {\n  const headers = new Headers(init.headers);\n  if (init.body) headers.set(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\");\n  const token = getToken();\n  if (token) headers.set(\"Authorization\", `Bearer ${token}`);\n  const res = await fetch(`/api${path}`, { ...init, headers });\n  const text = await res.text();\n  let payload: any = null;\n  try { payload = text ? JSON.parse(text) : null; } catch { payload = null; }\n  if (!res.ok) {\n    const message = payload && typeof payload.error === \"string\" ? payload.error : `Request failed (${res.status})`;\n    throw new ApiError(message, res.status);\n  }\n  return payload as T;\n}\n```\n\nAPI functions: register, login, logout, getMe, updateEmail, changePassword, requestPasswordReset, confirmPasswordReset, getOutbox.\n\nauth.tsx: AuthProvider with state { token, account }, loading state, signIn(username,password), signOut(), refresh().\n\nI'll store the token in localStorage under \"password-reset.token\".\n\nPages:\n\nHome:\n- If signed in: welcome card with link to account page, mailbox.\n- Else: two forms side by side: \"Create an account\" (username, password, optional email) and \"Sign in\" (username, password). Link \"Forgot your password?\" → /forgot-password.\n\nThe registration form: fields username, password, email (optional). On success: show success message \"Account created. Sign in below.\" and clear the form.\n\nSign-in form: on success, set token, navigate to /account? Or stay. I'll navigate to /account after sign-in. Hmm, a UI test might sign in and then expect to see the username in the header. Navigating to /account shows the account page. Both fine. I'll navigate to /account.\n\nForgotPassword page: form with username or email (single field \"username or email\"), submit → shows \"If the account exists, a reset link is on its way\". Also show a hint pointing to the Mailbox page.\n\nResetPassword page: reads token from ?token=. Shows the new password form with two fields (new password, confirm). If no token → show \"This reset link is missing its token.\" If success → \"Your password has been changed\" + link to sign in.\n\nAccount page: requires sign-in (if not signed in, show a prompt to sign in). Shows the email address, edit form (email input + save), change-password form (current, new, confirm).\n\nMailbox page: input for the address, \"Load\" button, lists messages (subject, sent_at, body with the link rendered as a clickable link). Newest first (API already sorts). Auto-fill the address from the signed-in user's email if available, and support ?to= query param.\n\nFor rendering the body, I should render newlines with `whitespace-pre-wrap`. And extract the reset link to render as an anchor. I'll parse the body for a URL via regex and render links.\n\nLet me write the code. I'll keep components in one file per page, plus a shared `ui.tsx` for Field/Label/Button components? Keep it simple: define small shared components in `src/components.tsx`.\n\nLet me write:\n\nsrc/components.tsx:\n```tsx\nexport function Card({title, children, description}) ...\nexport function LabeledInput(...)\nexport function Button({variant})\nexport function Alert({kind, children})\n```\n\nActually, to keep the code straightforward, I'll write a `ui.tsx` with: `TextField`, `SubmitButton`, `Message`, `Card`.\n\nTypeScript strictness: vite build doesn't typecheck by default (unless `tsc -b` is in the build script). The build script is just `vite build`. But there might be a tsconfig? Let me check the frontend dir for tsconfig.\n\nThere's no tsconfig.json in the listing! Only index.html, package-lock.json, package.json, src, vite.config.ts. So there's no tsconfig — Vite handles TS via esbuild without type checking. But react-router-dom types etc. still work at runtime. I should add a tsconfig.json for good practice and add a `typecheck` script? The Dockerfile only runs `npm run build`. Adding tsconfig.json is good practice — but if `vite build` doesn't typecheck, TS errors won't break the build. I'll add a tsconfig.json with standard Vite React TS settings so editors work, and optionally add \"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\" (requires typescript + @types/react as devDeps — more npm install weight). Hmm, adding typescript as a devDependency increases the Docker build time slightly but gives real type checking. Since the task mentions \"TypeScript\", having a proper tsconfig + types is good. Let me install typescript, @types/react, @types/react-dom as devDependencies and add a typecheck script. That's a reasonable, professional setup.\n\nLet me check what @types versions are compatible with React 19: @types/react@19.x, @types/react-dom@19.x.\n\nLet's install: npm i -D typescript @types/react @types/react-dom.\n\nThen create tsconfig.json:\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": true,\n    \"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\", \"DOM.Iterable\"],\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"verbatimModuleSyntax\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"noEmit\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"strict\": true,\n    \"noUnusedLocals\": true,\n    \"noUnusedParameters\": true,\n    \"noFallthroughCasesInSwitch\": true\n  },\n  \"include\": [\"src\", \"vite.config.ts\"]\n}\n```\nWith `verbatimModuleSyntax: true`, type imports must use `import type`. I'll be careful.\n\nAlso add \"build\": \"tsc -b && vite build\"? If the typecheck fails, the Docker build fails — risky but ensures quality. I'd rather keep \"build\": \"vite build\" (as the README states npm run build writes frontend/dist) and add a separate \"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\" script. Then I run typecheck locally to make sure it's clean. That's safer for the Docker build.\n\nLet me now write all frontend files.\n\nFirst install devdeps.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794314620831,"end":1794314620849}}}
