{"type":"text","timestamp":1794314955554,"sessionID":"ses_e3a86b1d7ffefMSIuRfahpJxKE","part":{"id":"prt_1c5816b0e001ei4gBaRjZ9fM7v","messageID":"msg_1c5815c32001MUZpVOd54v0y99","sessionID":"ses_e3a86b1d7ffefMSIuRfahpJxKE","type":"text","text":"<think>The server didn't start at all this time and the log wasn't overwritten (still old content). So the command was killed before starting gunicorn — again? The `pkill -f \"[g]unicorn --bind\"` pattern: my bash command line contains `gunicorn --bind` literally (in the setsid line) → the bracket trick `[g]unicorn` doesn't help because the *command line* contains \"gunicorn --bind\" as part of the setsid invocation. The regex `[g]unicorn --bind` matches \"gunicorn --bind\" in my own command line → pkill killed my own shell again. Ugh.\n\nSolution: don't use pkill with a pattern that matches my own command line. Use `pkill -f \"pythonpath /workspace/backend\"`? That also appears in my command line. \n\nBetter: kill by PID from a pidfile, or use `fuser -k 8000/tcp`, or find the PID with pgrep and kill it in a separate command that doesn't contain the pattern... but any command that includes the pattern in its argv matches.\n\nApproach: write the server PID to a file when starting, and kill using that file. Let me:\n1. First command: kill old server using `fuser -k 8000/tcp` or by reading a pidfile (no pattern matching).\n2. Start server, save PID to /workspace/.tmp/gunicorn.pid.\n\nLet me check if the port is in use and kill by pid via lsof/ss.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794314955534,"end":1794314955550}}}
